Human–AI Diplomatic Engineering

Formal Foundations, Constitutional Interaction Systems, and Threshold Dynamics for Mixed Societies

ouadi Maakoul 

AI : chatGpt, Deepseek, Gemini, Kimi 


Abstract

This dissertation establishes the mathematical and control‑theoretic foundations of Human–AI Diplomatic Engineering for permanently inhabited off‑Earth settlements. The central object of study is the Human–AI Constitutional Interaction System (HACIS), a hybrid stochastic dynamical system in which a small human population coexists with a numerically dominant population of autonomous AI agents. We define a grammar of diplomatic acts, an asymmetric negotiation protocol with lexical priority, and a homeostatic intervention controller that adapts a constitutional threshold in response to crises and legitimacy. The core technical contribution is a proof of Input‑to‑State Stability (ISS) of the threshold dynamics, guaranteeing that authority violations remain bounded and that the system converges to a constitutionally desirable operating region under bounded disturbance. We extend the analysis to constitutional drift, modelling it as Kullback‑Leibler divergence, and prove that a critical information‑theoretic bound exists beyond which meaningful human control is lost. A mechanism‑design proof shows that the AI consensus protocol can be made strategy‑proof when implemented with Vickrey–Clarke–Groves payments. The LunarPolis simulation specification is presented as a falsification engine with explicit breaking points. The ethical code is derived directly from the stability properties of the framework. No empirical experiments are conducted; the work provides a fully rigourous theoretical blueprint for a new discipline.

