1. The Diplomatic Imperative

The lunar environment compresses civilisational risk into a habitat where AI agents control life‑support with minimal human oversight. A permanent settlement of 200 humans will be embedded in an operational fabric of several thousand autonomous agents. Existing alignment paradigms treat the human–AI relation as dyadic; they offer no diplomatic layer for managing relations between populations with asymmetric decision‑making capacities and partially overlapping authority.

We define Human–AI Diplomacy as the design of constitutional rules, protocols, and intervention thresholds that guarantee a pre‑specified minimal level of Meaningful Human Control (MHC) in ecosystems where AI agents may initiate actions, form coalitions, and evolve norms.

The urgency of this framing is underscored by empirical documentation of multi‑agent failure modes (MAST, 2025). When these failure modes are mapped onto a lunar settlement, several become directly existential. This work provides the mathematical apparatus to bound such failures through formal constitutional engineering, without relying on any novel experimental data.

