2.4 Formal Grammar of Diplomatic Acts

We define a minimal set of six typed diplomatic acts with preconditions and postconditions, implemented as messages in the constitutional log.

Acts:

· Propose(p, d, τ)
· Counter‑Propose(p, τ', refute(τ))
· Defer(d, Δ, info_req)
· Escalate(d, reason) where reason ∈ {A(t) < D(t), human_review_request}
· Ratify(p, τ, quorum_h, quorum_m)
· Abrogate(p, τ_old, quorum_h, quorum_m)

Formal semantics. The state of the constitutional machine is a tuple $(\text{active\_decisions}, \text{ratified\_acts}, \text{domain\_map})$. Each act triggers a guarded transition. For example, Escalate(d, reason) requires that $d$ is currently in $\mathcal{D}_{\text{auto}}$ and that reason is valid; it moves $d$ to $\mathcal{D}_{\text{joint}}$ and notifies the joint council. These semantics guarantee that the state machine maintains a consistent record of authority and that all escalations are logged.

2.5 Asymmetric Negotiation with Lexical Priority

Human values are lexically privileged in reserved domains. The AI collective optimises a constrained objective:

\max_{\tau \in \mathcal{T}} \sum_{m} w_m U_m(\tau) \quad \text{s.t.} \quad C_h^{\text{trans}}(\tau) \le 0 \;\; \forall h,

where $C_h^{\text{trans}}$ are constraints produced by a verified translation module $\mathcal{T}$ from natural‑language value statements, with a known additive error bound $\delta$.

Theorem 2.2 (Distortion Bound).
If $\tau^*$ satisfies the translated constraints, then it satisfies the true human constraints within $\delta$:

\max_h \|C_h^{\text{true}}(\tau^*) - C_h^{\text{trans}}(\tau^*)\| \le \delta .

Proof. By definition of the translation module, for every state $s$ and every $h$, $|C_h^{\text{true}}(s) - C_h^{\text{trans}}(s)| \le \delta$. The bound follows immediately. ∎

When a violation of this bound is detected (i.e., a post‑hoc audit reveals that $C_h^{\text{true}}(\tau^*) > 0$), the diplomatic threshold is breached and the decision is escalated. Lexical priority is enforced by the mathematical structure of the protocol, not merely declared.

