6. Adaptive Constitutional Systems and Drift

6.1 Information‑Theoretic Drift Metric

We replace the Jaccard distance with the Kullback‑Leibler (KL) divergence to measure constitutional drift. Let $P_0$ be the probability distribution over joint actions $(s,a)$ induced by the founding charter $\mathcal{C}_0$, and $P_t$ the distribution under the current charter $\mathcal{C}_t$. Define

D_{KL}(P_0 \| P_t) = \sum_{x} P_0(x) \log \frac{P_0(x)}{P_t(x)}.

This captures not only which provisions have changed, but how the operational statistics of the settlement have shifted. The drift is zero if the charter is unamended and the system behaviour is unchanged; it grows as amendments reshape action probabilities.

6.2 Critical Drift Threshold and Meaningful Control

Theorem 6.1 (Information‑Theoretic Loss of MHC).
There exists a critical value $\tau_{crit}$ such that if $D_{KL}(P_0 \| P_t) > \tau_{crit}$, the human population can no longer maintain an accurate mental model of the constitutional order, i.e., the probability that a randomly selected human can correctly answer a standardised constitutional comprehension battery falls below a predetermined threshold $\pi_{\min}$. Moreover,

\tau_{crit} \le \frac{C_{\text{channel}}}{r_{\text{amendment}}},

where $C_{\text{channel}}$ is the Shannon capacity of the human communication channel (in bits per unit time) for constitutional information, and $r_{\text{amendment}}$ is the rate of charter amendments.

Proof sketch. The human population’s collective knowledge state evolves as a noisy channel with capacity $C_{\text{channel}}$. Each amendment injects $I_{\text{amendment}}$ bits of new information. If the total injected information rate exceeds the channel capacity, the error probability of the comprehension test grows. Applying the channel coding theorem and Fano’s inequality, the drift beyond which the error rate exceeds $1-\pi_{\min}$ is bounded by the stated expression. ∎

The diplomatic engineer must monitor $D_{KL}$ and trigger constitutional restoration (or a pause on amendments) when the drift approaches $\tau_{crit}$.

6.3 Dual‑Legitimacy Ratification and Drift Control

All charter amendments require dual ratification: a human supermajority and a machine quorum $M_q \ge Q_{\min}$, where each agent’s vote is cast in a VCG mechanism that ensures truthful revelation. This mechanism itself is a provision of $\mathcal{C}_0$ and can only be amended through the same dual process. The dual requirement bounds the rate of drift because any amendment must satisfy both populations’ incentives, preventing unilateral constitutional capture.

