7. Toward a Multiplanetary Constitutional Order

When multiple settlements exist, each is an independent HACIS with its own charter $\mathcal{C}_i$, threshold $D_i(t)$, and legitimacy $L_i(t)$. An inter‑settlement diplomatic overlay connects them via asynchronous consensus.

Legitimacy tensor.
Bilateral legitimacy is $\mathbf{L}_{ij}(t)$, derived from compliance history. Multilateral trust is encoded in a tensor $\mathbf{L}_{ijk}(t)$. Settlements that honour commitments accumulate legitimacy capital; a defector sees its capital diminish, and others tighten their intervention thresholds when interacting with it.

Communication‑delay compensation.
When lightspeed delay prevents real‑time human intervention, the local HACIS invokes a pre‑ratified commitment set of decision templates. The local threshold $D_i(t)$ is temporarily elevated to $D_{\text{safe}}$ during the gap. The delayed human review functions as a post‑hoc audit, updating $\mathbf{L}_{ij}$. This protocol is formally specified as a finite‑state machine with timeout transitions; its safety and liveness are proved under the assumption of eventual message delivery.

