Appendix A. Mathematical Preliminaries

A.1 Notation and conventions.
All vector spaces are Euclidean. For a function $f:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}^m$, $\|f\|$ denotes the supremum norm when applicable. $\mathbb{E}[\cdot]$ is the expectation operator. $\mathbf{1}_\mathcal{E}$ is the indicator of event $\mathcal{E}$. $L_2$-gain and $\mathcal{L}_\infty$ spaces are as defined in standard control texts.

A.2 Hybrid and Filippov systems.
A hybrid automaton is a tuple $(\mathcal{Q},\mathcal{X},\mathcal{F},\mathcal{G},\mathcal{R})$ where $\mathcal{Q}$ is a finite set of discrete modes, $\mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^n$ is the continuous state space, $\mathcal{F}(q,x)$ is a differential inclusion or vector field for each mode, $\mathcal{G}(q,q')$ are guard sets, and $\mathcal{R}(q,q',x)$ are reset maps. A solution is defined on a hybrid time domain. Because our controller contains discontinuous indicator functions, we consider Filippov solutions: for a differential equation $\dot{x}=f(x)$ with $f$ measurable and essentially bounded, the Filippov set-valued map is $K[f](x)=\bigcap_{\delta>0}\bigcap_{\mu(N)=0}\overline{\text{co}}\{f(B(x,\delta)\setminus N)\}$, and a solution is an absolutely continuous function satisfying $\dot{x}\in K[f](x)$ almost everywhere. This guarantees existence for the switched dynamics.

A.3 Input-to-State Stability (ISS).
Consider $\dot{x}=f(x,u)$ with $u\in\mathcal{U}\subset L_\infty$. The system is ISS if there exist $\beta\in\mathcal{KL}$ and $\gamma\in\mathcal{K}_\infty$ such that for any initial $x_0$ and any $u(\cdot)$,
\|x(t)\|\le\beta(\|x_0\|,t)+\gamma(\|u_{[0,t]}\|_\infty).


ISS implies robust stability and boundedness under bounded inputs. For Lyapunov characterisations, a smooth function $V:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}_{\ge0}$ is an ISS-Lyapunov function if there exist $\alpha_1,\alpha_2\in\mathcal{K}_\infty$, $\alpha_3\in\mathcal{K}$, and a positive definite $\alpha_4$ such that $\alpha_1(|x|)\le V(x)\le\alpha_2(|x|)$ and $\nabla V\cdot f(x,u)\le-\alpha_4(|x|)+\alpha_3(|u|)$.

A.4 Young's inequality and common bounds.
For any $a,b\ge0$ and $\theta>0$, $ab\le\frac{a^2}{2\theta}+\frac{\theta b^2}{2}$.

Appendix B. The HACIS Hybrid Automaton (Full Definition)

The HACIS is a stochastic hybrid automaton.

· Discrete modes: $Q=\{\text{Autonomous},\text{Intervention},\text{Crisis\_Resolution}\}$.
· Continuous state: $x=(H,A,D,L_h,L_m)\in\mathbb{R}^5$, constrained to $[0,1]^5$.
· Vector fields and differential inclusions:
  · In Autonomous mode, $\dot{D}$ follows the homeostatic law with $\mathbf{1}_{\text{viol}}$ determined by a violation detector; $A$ evolves via a consensus algorithm (stochastic); $H$ updates with each new proposal; legitimacy $L_h,L_m$ evolve slowly (see Appendix H).
  · In Intervention mode, $D$ is frozen, the human council deliberates, and $H$ is set to 1 for the escalated domain.
